# Chapter 12 · The Gender Critique of Structural Adjustment Policies

> "Discuss in detail the gender critique of Structural Adjustment Policies (SAPs)" (2022). The same ideas explain the effects of IMF programmes on Pakistani women today, a likely future question.

## 12.1  What SAPs were

In the late 1970s and 1980s many developing countries fell into a **debt crisis** (it broke out when Mexico could not pay in ==1982==). To get new loans from the **IMF** and **World Bank**, governments had to accept **conditions** — a package later called the "**Washington Consensus**" (a term coined by the economist **John Williamson** in ==1989==).

::: define Structural Adjustment Programmes (SAPs) | 1980s–1990s
Packages of economic reforms required by the IMF and World Bank in return for loans, aimed at reducing deficits, controlling inflation and opening economies to markets.
:::

::: table | The main SAP conditions.
@widths 4.4,9.6
Condition | What it means
Cut government spending | Reduce budgets for health, education, food subsidies and public jobs
Remove subsidies | End subsidies on food, fuel and electricity, so prices rise
User fees | Charge people for public health care and schooling
Devalue the currency | Make exports cheaper and imports (including food and medicine) dearer
Privatise | Sell state enterprises; cut public-sector jobs
Liberalise trade and finance | Remove tariffs and controls; promote exports
Deregulate labour markets | Weaken labour protections to make labour "flexible"
:::

## 12.2  Why SAPs were called "gender-blind"

SAPs were designed as if the economy had only **markets and the state**. They ignored the third part of the economy — the **household**, where unpaid care work keeps people alive. When the state cut services and prices rose, households had to fill the gap — and inside households the extra work fell on **women**.

::: thinker Diane Elson | *Male Bias in the Development Process* (1991)
Elson argued that adjustment policies contain **male bias**: they assume that women's unpaid work can expand without limit to absorb the costs of cuts — treating women's labour as if it were "**infinitely elastic**". Resources were meant to shift from "non-tradeable" to "tradeable" goods, but no one counted the shift of work from the paid economy to women's unpaid labour.
-> Elson's argument is the centre of the gender critique.
:::

::: fig flow | How structural adjustment shifted costs onto women.
@per 3
Cuts in health, education and subsidies
Prices of food, fuel and services rise
User fees for clinics and schools
Households must fill the gap
Women's unpaid work rises: caring, queuing, stretching food
Girls withdrawn from school to help; women's health suffers
:::

## 12.3  The gender critique, point by point

1. **Increased unpaid work.** When hospitals charged fees or discharged patients early, **women cared for the sick at home**. When subsidies ended, women spent more time stretching food and fuel.
2. **Women's health.** Cuts to health spending and user fees reduced women's use of maternal health services; maternal deaths rose in some countries.
3. **Girls' education.** School fees and household poverty led families to **withdraw girls** first, to save money and help at home.
4. **Loss of jobs.** Public-sector cuts removed jobs in teaching, nursing and administration where women were concentrated.
5. **Informal and insecure work.** Women were pushed into **informal work** — petty trade, home-based production — to make up lost income, often for very low returns.
6. **Export-led growth.** New export jobs (garments, electronics, flowers) went to women but were **low-paid, insecure and without labour rights**.
7. **Food prices.** Removal of food subsidies hit poor women, who are responsible for feeding families.
8. **Violence and stress.** Economic stress was linked with rising domestic tension and violence.
9. **Men lost the breadwinner role** in some places, shifting burdens onto women.
10. **No voice.** SAPs were negotiated by finance ministries and lenders with **no participation** by women.

## 12.4  The evidence and the response

- **UNICEF**'s *Adjustment with a Human Face* (**Cornia, Jolly and Stewart**, ==1987==) documented the harm to children and women and called for protection of the poor during adjustment.
- The **Commonwealth Secretariat**'s *Engendering Adjustment for the 1990s* (==1989==) showed the burden on women.
- **DAWN** (==1985==) placed the debt crisis at the centre of its critique.
- The IMF and World Bank responded with **social safety nets**, **Poverty Reduction Strategy Papers** (==1999==) and later "gender-responsive" language; critics say the core conditions changed little.

::: fig compare | The official view of SAPs and the gender critique.
## The official view
- Deficits and inflation must be cut
- Markets allocate resources efficiently
- Exports will create jobs and growth
- Short-term pain, long-term gain
## The gender critique
- Costs shifted to women's unpaid work
- Women's health and girls' schooling suffered
- New jobs insecure and low-paid
- Women had no voice in the decisions
:::

## 12.5  Structural adjustment in Pakistan

- Pakistan's first IMF structural adjustment programme began in ==1988==; there have been many since. The most recent include a **Stand-By Arrangement** (==2023==) and a **7-billion-dollar Extended Fund Facility** approved in ==2024==.
- Typical conditions: raising **electricity and gas prices**, widening the **sales tax**, cutting **development spending**, and reducing subsidies.
- **Gendered effects:** higher prices for food and energy (managed mainly by women); pressure on health and education budgets; fewer public jobs; rising informal work.
- **Protection:** the **Benazir Income Support Programme**, which pays women, has been **expanded** during IMF programmes as a social safety net — a partial answer to the gender critique, though cash transfers do not replace public services.

::: balance | Are IMF programmes anti-women?
- **Critics:** austerity shifts costs onto women's unpaid work; indirect taxes such as sales tax fall hardest on poor households; cuts hit health and education.
- **Defenders:** without stabilisation, inflation and default hurt the poor — and women — even more; recent programmes protect BISP spending.
-> The line to take: stabilisation may be necessary, but it must be **gender-assessed** — protect health, education and social protection, use progressive taxes, and count unpaid care.
:::

::: eye | The model structure for 2022
1. Introduction: debt crisis; SAPs defined; the gender critique in one line (Elson). 2. SAP conditions (table). 3. Why SAPs were gender-blind (the three-part economy). 4. The critique point by point — unpaid work, health, girls' schooling, jobs, informality, exports, food, violence, voice. 5. Evidence (UNICEF 1987; Commonwealth 1989; DAWN). 6. Official responses and their limits. 7. Pakistan (1988 onwards; 2024 EFF; BISP). 8. Conclusion: adjustment with a gender face. Draw the flow figure.
:::
